            <ArticleTitle>Giant aneurysmal fistula of the left main coronary artery to the right atrium.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>E292-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1532/HSF98.20121046</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 42-year-old woman presented to our outpatient department with complaints of atypical chest pain and palpitation. On physical examination, the patient's blood pressure was 140/95 mm Hg, and there was a systolic cardiac murmur on the left sternal border. A transthoracic echocardiography examination was performed, and a left-to-right shunt ratio (Qp/Qs) of approximately 1.5 was detected. Computed tomography angiography and coronary angiography examinations confirmed the presence of a large fistula between the left main coronary artery and the right atrium, with giant aneurysm formation and an intact right coronary artery. Surgical closure of the shunt was performed with a good final result.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
